From cb75bb6a109cedaba1f7346e53aadb04cac29e83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:26:47 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 31/34] arm64: dts: apple: t6050: enable the Neural Engine on
 J714c, J716s, J873s, K114s, K116c and K116s

The ADT of every one of these boards has the same ane0 as J714s: device
type ane, compatible "ane,t8132exclave", ane-type 0x220, die 0,
interrupts 954 and 967, clock ids 335, 334 and 381, and the dart-ane0
DARTs on interrupt 955. That is the engine t6050-peripherals.dtsi
describes, so enable it on them.

A board opts in by defining T6050_ANE before it includes
t6050-peripherals.dtsi, which then includes t6050-ane.dtsi at its end. This
keeps the change to one line at the top of each board file, away from the
lines other changes edit next to the peripherals include. The DTBs are the
same as with a direct include after t6050-peripherals.dtsi.

Nothing starts on its own. The boot loader enables ane0 and its
reservations only when the ADT says the firmware is preloaded and its
segments check out; otherwise it disables ane0. The driver then starts
the engine only when the preloaded TEXT matches a firmware build it knows,
and logs the digest and leaves the engine alone when it does not.

Left out here:
 - J716c, K114c and J775c, which have their own open changes for this.
 - J775d (M5 Ultra): the second die has its own ane1, DARTs and power
   state, none of which are described, and the boot loader fills only
   /arm-io/ane0.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>

[re-cut onto the K114s r3d tree]
